    "https://ap.washington.edu/about-us/faculty-affairs-advisory-committee/": {
      "title": "Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee",
      "description": "Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty About Us Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee The FAAC serves as the advisory board to Faculty Affairs in the UW Tri-Campus System. The Committee primarily focuses on operations and procedures regarding faculty affairs throughout the UW Tri-Campus system. FAAC provides overall direction, priority setting, guidance for process improvement and development of new initiatives and programming. During meetings, the advisory committee discusses: implementation strategies, best practices, and other issues related to faculty development, promotion, merit and unit adjustment, faculty annual reviews, excellence, equity, and assist in the communication process by sharing information discussed at college/school, and campus levels. The inaugural chair of FAAC is Deepa Rao and Trish Kritek is the co-chair. The committee meets once a month. FAAC representatives College/School/Campus / Services Representatives Arts & Sciences, College of Maya Smith Associate Dean for Equity, Justice and Inclusion mayaas@uw.edu Built Environments, College of Ann Marie Borys Associate Dean for Academic Affairs amborys@uw.edu Dentistry, School of Whasun Chung Associate Dean for Faculty sochung@uw.edu Education, College of Carol Davis Associate Dean for Academic Affairs cadavis1@uw.edu Engineering, College of Aileen Trilles Senior Director of Human Resources avt@uw.edu The Environment, College of Stephanie Harrington Associate Dean for Administration and Finance stephah@u w.edu Evans School of Public Policy and Governance David F. Suarez Associate Dean for Research dsuarez@uw.edu u Foster School of Business Jennifer Koski Associate Dean for Academic and Faculty Affairs jkoski@uw.edu Information School Katie Davis Associate Dean for Faculty Affairs kdavis78@uw.edu Law, School of Elizabeth Pendo Associate Dean for Academic Administration ependo@uw.edu Libraries Laura Lillard Director of Libraries Human Resources lill@uw.edu Medicine, School of Giana Davidson, Interim Vice Dean for Faculty Affairs ghd@uw.edu Ashlee Harty, Associate Dean for Academic Affairs and Medical Staff Affairs hartya@uw.edu Nursing, School of Hilaire Thompson Dean of Nursing hilairet@uw.edu Pharmacy, School of Jing Li Associate Professor jli0321@uw.edu Public Health, School of Stephanie Farquhar Associate Dean for Faculty farqs@uw.edu Social Work, School of Jennie Romich Associate Dean for Academic Affairs romich@uw.edu UW Bothell Wadiya Udell Assistant Vice Chancellor for Faculty Success wau2@uw.edu UW Tacoma Sarah Davies Breen Director of Faculty Affairs & Academic Human Resources sdaviesb@uw.edu × Home About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Fredrick M. Nafukho, PhD, MDP Marjorie Olmstead, PhD Deepa Rao, PhD Chadwick Allen, PhD Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/faculty-development/": {
      "title": "Faculty Development",
      "description": "Faculty Development - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Faculty Development Academic Personnel & Faculty Faculty Development Cultivating Excellence Across the Academic Journey At the University of Washington, we believe faculty growth drives academic excellence and institutional impact. Our Faculty Development programs foster individual success and organizational change through tailored workshops, leadership training, mentorship, and curated resources that support teaching, research, service, and clinical work. Grounded in continuous learning and collaboration, we ensure faculty feel supported, connected, and empowered to achieve their goals. Whether starting an academic career or stepping into leadership, we provide the tools, relationships, and guidance needed to thrive. New Academic Leader Onboarding The New Academic Leader Onboarding program, led by the Office for Academic Personnel & Faculty, supports UW faculty stepping into leadership roles. Through in-person and virtual sessions, speakers offer guidance on topics like equity, governance, and financial planning, providing a well-rounded foundation for success in academic leadership. See 2025 Highlights Promotion Workshops Offered throughout the year, Promotion Workshops equip faculty with the tools and guidance needed for a successful promotion. Designed for faculty at all stages, the session covers dossier preparation, promotion criteria, and effective presentation of accomplishments. Attendees hear directly from academic leaders and recently promoted peers, with time for questions and practical advice. Resources are provided to support continued planning and progress. Learn More Leadership Success Leadership Success is a collaborative initiative led by the Faculty Development team in partnership with Faculty Inclusive Excellence, Faculty Affairs, and colleagues across all three UW campuses. Currently in development, this effort is designed to create a connected and comprehensive network of resources and peers that supports the ongoing success of department chairs and other academic leaders. Mentoring the Mentor Dean & Chancellor Leadership Series The Dean and Chancellor Leadership Development Series is a professional development and support program designed for deans, chancellors, divisional deans, associate deans and associate vice chancellors, addressing topics of shared importance to department and unit leaders. This collaborative series is led by the office for Academic Personnel and Faculty and provides an engaging platform to explore essential themes in leadership and academia. Learn More Postdoctoral Scholars In recognition of their expertise and contributions to the UW community, APF is invested in Postdoctoral Scholars’ professional and personal development from appointment and onboarding, through career advancement and beyond. The linked landing page provides curated resources for postdocs, faculty advisors, and unit administrators.  The landing page is organized around key themes including administrative affairs, professional development, and upcoming events. Explore the sub-pages to find the guidance most relevant to your role. Learn More Additional Resources A variety of leadership development opportunities are available for faculty and academic leaders at all career stages. As a member of the Big Ten Academic Alliance, the UW provides access to prestigious external programs including the Academic Leadership Program (ALP) , the Department Executive Officer Program (DEO) , and the Deans Leadership Program (DLP) . In addition, the UW sponsors the Conflict Engagement and Resolution Training —an interactive program that equips faculty and leaders with tools to navigate challenging conversations and promote collaborative academic environments. Each program enhances skills in management, communication, conflict resolution, faculty development, and strategic decision-making through shared expertise. × University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/faculty-inclusive-excellence/": {
      "title": "Faculty Inclusive Excellence",
      "description": "Faculty Inclusive Excellence - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Faculty Inclusive Excellence Academic Personnel & Faculty Faculty Inclusive Excellence Academic Personnel & Faculty Inclusive Excellence programming aims to provide University of Washington campus, college, school, and unit leadership with the tools and resources necessary to enhance excellence through broad representation and to build and retain a more inclusive faculty. Announcements New Required Training: Learn about the new APF Required Training for Faculty Search Committees . Become an NCFDD Member for Free: Leverage Resources for Faculty , through the University of Washington’s Institutional Membership. About Inclusive Excellence Inclusive Excellence promotes the hiring, retention, and success of a diverse and inclusive faculty at the University of Washington. Learn more Faculty Search Required Training and Best Practices for hiring a diverse and inclusive faculty across the three campuses. Valuable resources including the Faculty Search Handbook and Toolkit. Faculty Search Resources Faculty Search Webinars & Workshops Training opportunities for developing effective and inclusive search strategies. Find more information about available workshops and how to request a search committee webinar. Find Faculty Search Webinars & Workshops Resources for Leadership Programs and initiatives for mentorship, leadership development, networking, work life balance, and institutional support. Find Resources for Leaders Resources for Faculty Opportunities for mentorship, career and leadership development, networking, wellness and work-life balance. Find Resources for Faculty Articles & Reports Discover informative publications and reports for department chairs, faculty job candidates, and mid-career faculty to support your academic journey. Find Articles & Reports Contact Us Chadwick Allen, Ph.D. Associate Vice Provost for Academic Personnel & Faculty Inclusive Excellence callen3@uw.edu (206) 616-7044 Joyce Yen, Ph.D. Director, UW ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change joyceyen@uw.edu (206) 543-3605 Comail Rizvi, M.S. Program Operations Specialist, Academic Personnel & Faculty Inclusive Excellence sr1591@uw.edu (206) 685-0151 Contact us at : facadv@uw.edu × Home Faculty Inclusive Excellence About Inclusive Excellence Faculty Search APF Required Faculty Search Committee Training Handbook of Best Practices for Faculty Searches Online Toolkit for Faculty Searches Faculty Search Webinars and Workshops Request a Search Committee Workshop Resources for Leadership New Leaders Program Resources for Faculty Articles & Reports University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/working/faculty-review-resources/": {
      "title": "Chair-Faculty Conference",
      "description": "Faculty Review Resources - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs For Academic Personnel & Faculty Faculty Review Resources Faculty Review Resources The University of Washington Faculty Code establishes a framework of regular faculty reviews designed to support professional growth, promote open communication, and ensure fair and informed evaluation processes. Faculty Code Section 24-57 requires ongoing faculty reviews that assess teaching effectiveness through student evaluations and collegial review, along with regular conferences to discuss progress, goals, expectations, and professional development. Review frequency varies by rank and appointment type, with annual reviews for assistant professors and pre-tenure faculty and periodic reviews for other ranks. Written documentation of reviews is shared with faculty members to support transparency, development, and personnel decisions. The resources below provide templates and guidance to assist departments, academic units, and faculty members in carrying out these important responsibilities. Collegial Course Review The Faculty Code requires that the teaching effectiveness of every faculty member be evaluated by colleagues, consider all five core elements of effective teaching , and may incorporate evaluation procedures established within the department, school, or college. These collegial reviews complement student evaluations by providing discipline-specific, professional assessment of teaching practices, course design, instructional methods, and student engagement. Review frequency varies by rank; however, annual reviews are encouraged for all faculty, and a collegial review is required within one year of a promotion review. The Collegial Course Review resource page provides an overview of the review process, the core elements of effective teaching, and a review template. By promoting thoughtful peer feedback, collegial reviews support instructional excellence, faculty development, and student success. Collegial Course Review Resources Chair-Faculty Conference The Faculty Code also requires regular individual conferences between faculty members and their chair, dean, or designee. These conversations are intended to support career planning and professional development and are distinct from merit review processes. During the conference, faculty members and academic leaders discuss: The faculty member’s current teaching, scholarly, and service responsibilities and accomplishments. Departmental needs, priorities, and strategic goals. Shared goals for the coming review period. Strategies for achieving those goals. Expectations, responsibilities, available resources, and opportunities for support. The Chair-Faculty Conference Template provides a framework for documenting these discussions and capturing agreed-upon goals, development plans, and support needs. When used effectively, the chair conference strengthens communication, clarifies expectations, and helps ensure that faculty members have the guidance and resources necessary to thrive throughout their academic careers. VIEW CHAIR-FACULTY CONFERENCE TEMPLATE Faculty Conference with Department Chair Template × Download Template Faculty Conference with Department Chair Template All department chairs are required to conduct faculty conferences annually with faculty per the schedule for each faculty rank and guidelines given in the faculty code (see Section 24-55 ). This document is meant as a guide for department chairs on a) meeting discussion content and b) written documentation of these reviews. Units should adapt this template to their local needs, adding categories for assessment and tracking. In the annual review, the chair, dean, or designee and the faculty member discuss and identify any specific duties and responsibilities expected of, and resources available to, the faculty member during the coming year(s), taking into account the academic functions described in Section 24-32 . The chair, dean, or designee should make specific suggestions, as necessary, to improve or aid the faculty member’s work. Prior to review, at minimum, department chairs ask each faculty member to submit updated curriculum vita. As well, the faculty code outlines review across three categories: Scholarship (e.g. research, practice, and teaching scholarly products), Teaching (e.g. classroom-, clinic-, field-based teaching), and Service (e.g. University, Community, Professional, Leadership, Inclusive Excellence). Review Context Department Chair ___________________________ Faculty Member _____________________________ Academic Position / Rank ____________________ Date of Review _____________________________ Scholarship Activity (e.g. Books, Papers, Conference Presentations, Community Workshop/Roundtable Agendas, Awards, Other Scholarly Output) Key Accomplishments Growth areas / Career Development Teaching Please see updated text in faculty code ( Section 24-32 , dated February 18, 2025) and background from faculty senate . Activity (list of formal/informal teaching roles and dates, evaluation, response to evaluation; students mentored/graduated) Key Accomplishments Growth areas / Career Development Service Activity (roles and dates) Key Accomplishments Growth areas / Career Development Overall Issues Comments Faculty Conference with Department Chair Template Together, collegial reviews and chair conferences create a collaborative process that supports individual success, strengthens academic units, and advances the University’s mission of excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. Home For Academic Personnel & Faculty Faculty Review Resources Collegial Course Review New Faculty @UW New Faculty Onboarding Academic Responsibilities Instructional Responsibility Policy International Remote Work for Academic Personnel Off-Site Work Guidelines for Faculty and Postdoctoral Scholars Safety in the Workplace Faculty Promotion & Tenure Promotion Workshops Case Contents Crafting Your CV & Self-Assessment The Path to Promotion Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Promotion and Tenure Clock Waivers 2026-27 Promotion and Tenure Clock Waiver: One Year Extension 2025-26 Promotion and Tenure Clock Waiver: One Year Extension Promotion/Tenure Clock Extensions Due to COVID-19 for Faculty Promotion Clock Extensions Due to COVID-19 for Librarians Leaving the UW Retiring from the UW Working After Retirement Resigning from the UW University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
