    "https://ap.washington.edu/postdoctoral-scholars/": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholars",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholars - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Postdoctoral Scholars Postdoctoral Scholars Welcome! The Office for Academic Personnel & Faculty (APF) recognizes and values the contributions of postdoctoral scholars to the UW community. The goal of these pages is to connect postdoctoral scholars, their faculty advisors, and unit administrators with the resources they need, whether it’s navigating policies or developing as scholars and professionals. In the menu to the right, you can find resources for UW-affiliated postdoctoral scholars, their faculty advisors, and unit administrators.  In those pages, we have curated resources organized around the themes of administrative affairs and employment and professional life.  Below, you can find information on upcoming events from around UW. This page is actively maintained by the Faculty Development unit of APF. Events of interest The posts below highlight events, workshops, and opportunities of interest to the postdoctoral community. Check back periodically for updates. Virtual Career Fair Dates Announced August 20, 2026 Postdocs - Save the Dates Registration is open for the 2026 National Career Fair Series! The series is composed of three nationwide virtual recruitment events for postdoctoral scholars to find career opportunities in industry, government, and academia. All fairs run from 12:00-4:00 p.m. Eastern Time: Industry & government positions: N-GSPIRE fair on September 21 Postdoc… Read more Save the Date for Postdocs & Advisors: Mentoring Undergrads August 11, 2026 Dates for the 2026 fall series on mentoring undergraduate researchers have been announced. The first on \"Getting to Know Your Undergraduate Research Resources\" has been scheduled twice so you can choose the format that works best for you: Monday, October 12, 2026 // 12:00-1:00 PM – in person, Seattle campus Tuesday, October 27, 2026 //… Read more BTAA Career Fair Concludes May 29, 2026 Big Ten Virtual Job Fair for Postdocs Concludes The Big Ten Academic Alliance (BTAA) held its 2026 virtual Academic Postdoc Hiring Fair on March 25.  This event brought together postdoctoral researchers and PhDs with hiring teams looking to fill faculty and postdoctoral positions from across the now 18+ Big Ten institutions.  Held on the career… Read more Dates of Upcoming Events Please update your browser to view the iframe content. × Home Postdoctoral Scholars Postdoctoral Scholar Administrative Affairs Postdoctoral Scholar Resources University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=190338": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Teaching-Track, Information Systems and Information Management",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Assistant Teaching Professor, Teaching-Track, Information Systems and Information Management Position Overview School / Campus / College: Information School Organization: Information School Title: Assistant Teaching Professor, Teaching-Track, Information Systems and Information Management Position Details Position Description The Information School at the University of Washington invites applications for two teaching faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor, with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in teaching and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis for a nine-month academic, 3 year appointment beginning 2027. The base salary range for this position will be $11,500 - 13,390 per month, commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. The successful candidate will be expected to design curriculum and deliver courses across the degree programs offered at the Information School, including our Master of Information Management (MSIM) and Bachelor of Informatics. This position will be expected to teach in one or more of the following topics: Databases Data Visualization Enterprise Systems and Data Management Positive factors for consideration include, but are not limited to, candidates who have experience applying their expertise in one or more of the following areas: Artificial Intelligence; Data Science; Information Architecture; Cybersecurity and Information Assurance. About the University of Washington Information School Information is central to knowledge; to understanding; to impact. Amid the AI transformation, climate change and ongoing threats to public and civic health, information plays a pivotal role in giving everyone the tools to thrive. Our work advances effective information systems and equitable access to information by transforming practices within organizations large and small, and influencing public policy. The UW Information School is leading the global movement to devise information solutions that enrich people’s lives and tackle the world’s toughest challenges through our in-demand undergraduate and graduate level education and our interdisciplinary research centers and partnerships. Together, our students, faculty, alumni and partners are actively creating a future where effective use of information can help everyone discover, learn, innovate, solve problems, and have fun. Information changes lives. Learn more at ischool.uw.edu. Qualifications Applicants for this teaching faculty position must meet the following minimum qualifications: Applicants must minimally have a master’s degree (or foreign equivalent). Applicants must have 3 years of experience in a technical role in industry, government, or a nonprofit, or experience teaching at least one course as either the lead or assistant instructor. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than October 15, 2026. Priority will be given to those applications received by October 15, 2026. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com . The position will remain open until filled. Applications should include the following materials: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter - Describe briefly your academic and professional background. Please state specifically your expertise in one or more of the following areas: databases, data visualization, or enterprise systems (2 pages maximum) Teaching Statement - Describe your approach to and experience teaching, in particular at the undergraduate or graduate level. Where appropriate, please include any experience designing courses, instructional materials, or curriculum development. (3 pages maximum) Throughout the materials above, applicants should articulate how their past experiences and/or future plans in research, teaching, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Please also include contact information for 2 referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=190339": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Library Information Science",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Library Information Science Position Overview School / Campus / College: Information School Organization: Information School Title: Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Library Information Science Position Details Position Description The Information School at the University of Washington invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in research, teaching, and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis for a nine-month academic appointment, with a 3-year term, renewable beginning 2027. The base salary range for this position will be $13,000 - $14,935 per month, commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. The successful candidate will be expected to: Conduct high-quality research, and pursue extramural funding as needed to support research; Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of specialization; Perform service that furthers the mission of the Information School and the University of Washington more broadly; and Contribute to the Information School’s strategic areas: climate change, health and wellness, healthy information environments, and responsibility in artificial intelligence. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to our Library and Information Science (LIS) research, with areas of relevance including, but not limited to: Libraries - Academic, Digital, or Public Librarianship - Leadership, Innovation, or Education Information Technology - Ethics, Public Interest, and Justice-Oriented Approaches Data - Archives, Public / Open, and Humanistic or Scientific Applications Artificial Intelligence - Public Interest, Library or Cultural Heritage-Centered, Open-Source Applications in Information Institutions About the University of Washington Information School Information is central to knowledge; to understanding; to impact. Amid the AI transformation, climate change and ongoing threats to public and civic health, information plays a pivotal role in giving everyone the tools to thrive. Our work advances effective information systems and equitable access to information by transforming practices within organizations large and small, and influencing public policy. The UW Information School is leading the global movement to devise information solutions that enrich people’s lives and tackle the world’s toughest challenges through our in-demand undergraduate and graduate level education and our interdisciplinary research centers and partnerships. Together, our students, faculty, alumni and partners are actively creating a future where effective use of information can help everyone discover, learn, innovate, solve problems, and have fun. Information changes lives. Learn more at ischool.uw.edu. All University of Washington faculty are expected to engage in teaching, research, and service. Qualifications Applicants for this position must meet the following minimum qualifications: Applicants must minimally hold a PhD (or foreign equivalent). Applicants must have 3 years of experience in conducting research, inclusive of graduate studies. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than October 30, 2026. Priority will be given to those applications received by October 30, 2026. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com . The position will remain open until filled. Applications should include the following materials: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter - Describe briefly your academic and professional background, your areas of research expertise, and your teaching interests. (1 page maximum) Research Statement - Describe your research agenda including your major work to date, your plans for future research, and the sources of potential funding for your research into the future. (3 pages maximum) Teaching Statement - Describe your approach to and experience teaching, in particular  at the undergraduate or graduate level. Where appropriate, please include any experience designing courses, instructional materials, or curriculum development. (2 pages maximum) Throughout the materials above, applicants should articulate how their past experiences and/or future plans in research, teaching, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Please also include contact information for 3 referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
