    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=188520": {
      "title": "Acting Instructor – Mechanistic Computational Modelling",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Acting Instructor – Mechanistic Computational Modelling Position Overview School / Campus / College: School of Medicine Organization: Bioengineering (SOM) Title: Acting Instructor – Mechanistic Computational Modelling Position Details Position Description The Department of Bioengineering at the University of Washington School of Medicine invites applications for a n Acting Instructor position with an anticipated start date of December 16 , 2026 . The successful candidate will be appointed full-time for a 12-month appointment. Acting Instructor appointments are annual and may be renewed based on performance and the availability of funding. This position is being offered at a salary range of $ 5,417 to $ 6,667 per month ( $ 65,004 – $80,004 per year), commensurate with experience. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct i ndependent p roject d evelopment , including the following : Lead the design and execution of computational biology projects , like mechanistic modeling of vascular-cellular physiology . Expected approaches include but not limited to mass ‑ action kinetic models, performing sensitivity and uncertainty analyses, and conducting rigorous statistical analysis of experimental and clinical datasets. The candidate should demonstrate broad knowledge of receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) signaling (and related pathways) and be able to translate biological hypotheses into quantitative models that can be tested against data. They will play a leading role in intellectual development activities, independently drive key portions of research projects, and are expected to publish as first author in high ‑ impact , peer ‑ reviewed journals, in addition to serving as co ‑ author on collaborative publications, presentations, and reports. Funding Contribution: Identifies funding sources and contributes to developing new proposals. Candidates are expected to actively pursue independent research funding and build a long-term trajectory toward securing grants as PI or co-PI. Mentoring: Mentors students and junior researchers in the development of technical and professional skills. Qualifications Applicants for this Acting Instructo r p osition must meet the following minimum qualifications: PhD degree in a related quantitative field (e.g., bioengineering, biomedical engineering, computational biology, biophysics, chemical engineering, applied mathematics, computer science, or a closely related discipline), Expertise in receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) signaling (and related pathways), Strong record of scientific writing with experience publishing as first author in high-impact, peer-reviewed journals, and At least two years of postdoctoral experience in computational modeling of biological systems. This includes programming (e.g., Python, R, MATLAB), quantitative data analysis, and development and application of mechanistic , deterministic models. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than October 1, 2026 . Priority will be given to those applications received by October 1, 2026 . Applications will only be accepted via University of Washington's Interfolio. The position will remain open until filled. Applications should include the following materials: A cover letter of no more than one (1) page detailing your interest in the position, relevant qualifications, and experiences A curriculum vitae Please also include contact information for at least three (3) referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request. Applicants should articulate how their past experiences and/or future plans in research, teaching, mentoring, patient care, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Contact Dr. Imoukhuede and Dr. Fang at Imoukhuedelab@u.washington.edu .  More information about the Imoukhuede Systems Biology Lab can be found at https://sites.bioe.uw.edu/imoukhuede/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
